@model ProductListViewModel

<h1>@Model.PageTitle</h1>


<table class="table">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th>Id</th>
            <th>Name</th>
            <th>Price</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        @foreach (var product in Model.Products)
        {
            <tr>
                <td>@product.Id</td>
                <td>@product.Name</td>
                <td>&pound;@product.Price.ToString()</td>
            </tr>
        }
    </tbody>
</table>
<div>
    <h3>Explanation</h3>
    <p>
        This action method, `ViewModel`, is an MVC controller endpoint that prepares data for a Razor view. It creates
        an
        instance of `ProductListViewModel`, which acts as a container for all data the page needs, instead of passing
        loose
        values through `ViewBag` or `ViewData`. This is the strongly-typed approach and is generally easier to maintain.
    </p>
    <p>
        Inside the object initializer, `PageTitle` is set to `"Products"` and `Products` is populated with three product
        objects. The `new()` syntax relies on target-typed object creation, so the compiler infers the product type from
        the
        `Products` collection type in `ProductListViewModel`. The `m` suffix on prices (for example, `999.99m`) ensures
        each
        value is treated as a `decimal`, which is preferred for currency to avoid floating-point precision issues.
    </p>
    <p>
        Finally, `return View(model);` sends that populated view model to the Razor view. By convention, MVC will look
        for a
        view named `ViewModel.cshtml` under `Views/DataToViews/` (or `Views/Shared/`). If the file uses a different
        name,
        the view name must be passed explicitly, such as `View("productListViewModel", model)`.
    </p>
    <p>
        It is in ViewModels because its job is UI-specific, not domain-specific.
    </p>
    <p>
        In this project, ProductListViewModel.cs shapes data exactly for a Razor page: title + list of products in a
        format
        the view expects. That is different from a core Model, which usually represents business/domain data or
        persistence
        entities used across the app.
    </p>
    <p>
        A good rule of thumb:
        <ol>
            <li>Models: business meaning, reused across layers, not tied to one page.</li>
            <li>ViewModels: tailored to one screen/action, may combine multiple sources, includes display-focused fields.
            </li>
        </ol>
    </p>
    <p>
        Keeping them separate avoids coupling your domain objects to UI concerns and makes refactoring views safer. In
        small
        apps you can place both under a single Models folder, but separate ViewModels is a common and clean MVC
        convention.
    </p>

</div>